<title>USA: IMF's NAB to draw from 13 new nations - Tietmeyer.</title>
<headline>IMF's NAB to draw from 13 new nations - Tietmeyer.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Bundesbank president Hans Tietmeyer said on Sunday that 13 countries had agreed to contribute funds to a new borrowing facility for the International Monetary Fund.</p>
<p>He listed the 13 new contributor nations as Australia, Austria, Belgium, Denmark, Finland, Hong Kong, (South) Korea, Luxembourg, Malta, Norway, Singapore, Spain and Thailand.</p>
<p>He declined to give details on how much each country planned to contribute.  </p>
<p>The new fund, to be called the New Arrangements to Borrow, will double the size of an existing $25 billion facility offered to the Fund by the Group of 10 nations, the General Arrangements to Borrow.</p>
<p>The new NAB will exist alongside the GAB, with G10 nations contributing 80 percent of the total NAB funds and a further 13 nations putting up the remaining 20 percent, he said.</p>
<p>Tietmeyer told reporters that an agreement in principle had been reached on the terms for the deal but that final details had to be hammered out.</p>
<p>The Group of 10 comprises Belgium, Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan the Netherlands, Sweden, Switzerland and the United States</p>
<p>--Janet Northcote, Wsahington Newsroom +1 202 898 8300</p>
